1 NOTE {geni:about_me} IRENE HAUSER, WORKED TO AID REFUGEES St. Paul Pioneer Press (MN)  |  19 February 1990
2 CONT
2 CONT A memorial service will be held today for Irene Melgaard Hauser, a founder of the Minnesota International Center and the organizer of its first visiting foreign leader program.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died Saturday in Minneapolis at age 93.
2 CONT
2 CONT The service will be at 2 p.m. at Plymouth Congregational Church, 1900 Nicollet Ave. in Minneapolis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hauser, a native of Argyle, majored in English at Carleton College in Northfield and graduated magna cum laude in 1918. She was the first woman editor of the college newspaper, the Carletonian.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1925, she married Walter Hauser, a Minneapolis lawyer and community leader. He died in 1975.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before and during World War II, she worked with the American Legion Auxiliary and its Council of Americanization helping European refugees in Minneapolis.
2 CONT
2 CONT She also was active in Minnesota Church Women United, which evolved into the Minnesota International Center in 1953. She was volunteer coordinator for the visiting foreign leaders program.
2 CONT
2 CONT When her husband was appointed honorary consul for West Germany in 1957, Hauser helped organize activities for scores of guests brought to Minneapolis by the U.S. and West German governments.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1960, Gov. Orville Freeman named Hauser Minnesota Mother of the Year. In 1965, she received the Distinguished Alumna Award from Carleton College.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is survived by two daughters, three sisters, two grandchildren and one great-granddaughter.
