1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hans and his Melgaard Cousins
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans Myhre, Sue’ Great Grandfarther, was the son of Erik and Eli Myhre/Segelstad.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Erik had a twin brother Lars who married Anne Melgaard, living on the Melgaard gard (farm) and took that surname.  Lars died at an early age, so Erik took in his family.  Of their nine children, I believe seven immigrated to Ameri
2 CONC ca. One daughter Karen who stayed in Norway had a son Leif Melgaard who emigrated and is accounted for in the next article. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is a biography of the Melgaards, the cousins referenced in the history of Hans Myhre who as early pioneers in the upper mid-west became bankers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans L. Melgaard:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans at the of 14 worked in his uncle Erik’s store for three years, while Hans Myhre attended the County or District School.  He then moved to Lillehammer for 4 years, and then came to America in 1882 and went directly to Fargo, t
2 CONC hen Dakota Territory. He worked as a bookkeeper for 3 ½ years, moved to Crookston and became a partner in an insurance loan office.  After a year he moved to Argyle and opened the first bank there in 1886, The Farmers and Merchant
2 CONC  Bank.  His brother Ole became the cashier. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the publication, “A Self Portrait of Marshall County” there is this reference:
2 CONT
2 CONT Early days of Argyle:  By the mid-1880’s Hans Melgard (not spelling change) had arrived to open a bank on the rear lot leased to J.J. Stone for a granary.  In 1891, a fire broke out during a show storm and an entire block of busin
2 CONC esses south of the Melgard bank were gone.  Several years later Hans and Ole Melgard teamed up to build the Farmers and Merchants Bank.  Hans Melgards bank building was later purchased, moved to a new lot, and transformed into a p
2 CONC ost office.  In 1905 a grain elevator and potato warehouse became the property of the Farmers and Merchants Company and was reported to be doing a flourishing business.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole L. Melgaard:
2 CONT
2 CONT He left Norway in 1876 with an uncle to America, landing in Philadelphia during the Exposition and then continued on to Black River Falls.  He finished his education and learned the English language while working on his uncle’s fa
2 CONC rm.  He then worked in La Crosse, WI., married Julia, and moved to Argyle.  In 1889 he became cashier of the Farmers State Bank of which his oldest brother was President.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew L. Melgaard
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1887 he came to America by sailing vessel settling at La Crosse, WI, where his brother Ole was employed.  He attended school in that city and in Black River Falls to perfect his English, and in 1888 set out for Fa
2 CONC rgo, Dakota Territory, where he secured employment with the Fargo Loan Agency and joined the Dakota Territorial Guard.  In 1892 he came to Warren to become an assistant cashier in the State Bank of Warren that had just been organi
2 CONC zed by his brother Hans.  In 1902 he sold his interest in the State Bank of Warren and moved to Washington and Idaho where he purchases and then sold banks.  In 1910 he purchased the Woodward or commonly known Snake River Farm nor
2 CONC th of Warren, on of the large “bonanza” farms in the Red River Valley, which he operated until his death in 1964.  He married Minnie Carper in 1894 and they had eight children, one being Lloyd George who knew Sue’s grandparents, E
2 CONC va and Ben very well and also supplied the family with much information on our Norwegian relations.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Lloyd George after being discharged from the Army in 1952 lived and operated the Melgaard farm until his retirement in 1968.  At one time he was President of the local historical society and published a book Warrem, Plains to Plen
2 CONC ty: A Story of 100 Eventful Years for the centennial celebration in 1980.  He published a family history The Carper Family Tree for his mother's family.  
2 CONT
2 CONT  There is also reference to another brother, Torger L. Melgaard, born in Norway in 1873, banker in Marshall County, but have no additional information at this time. There is one reference to him in Thief River Falls (see Eva Myhre
2 CONC  section)
2 CONT
2 CONT   Note that all of these Melgaard’s used (L) Lars as their middle name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1917 the following names are listed with the Farmers State Bank of Newfolden:
2 CONT
2 CONT O. L Melgaard, President, H. L Melgaard, Vice President; N. E Myhre, Cashier, J. H. Johnson, Assistant Cashier.  Directors—O.  L. Melgaard, H. L Melgaard, both of Argyle; T. L. Melgaard, Thief River Falls; Andr. Korstad, H. E. Myh
2 CONC re, both of Newfoldent.  At the time they had $163,677.33 in assets and liabilities. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hawkin L. Melgard 
2 CONT
2 CONT Another brother, Hawkin L. Melgard (Hakon in Norway) is found in the Who's Who in Finance and Banking written in 1922.  Hawkin received his public high school education in Norway and arrived in the US in 1898.  He first was a cler
2 CONC k at the Bank of Erskine, MN, then assistant cashier Scandia State Bank of Stephen, MN, and finally cashier and president of the First Trust and Savings Bank of Moscoe, Idaho, in 1906.  He also became the mayor, president of the M
2 CONC oscow Board of Education, Treasurer of the Chamber of Commerce and member of many fraternal organizations.  
