1 NAME Jacob Hansen /Myhre/
2 GIVN Jacob Hansen
2 SURN Myhre
2 _MARNM Myhre
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1 MAR 1843
2 PLAC Øvre Myhre Ringebu Oppland Norway
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 16 JAN 1925
2 PLAC Willow, Griggs, North Dakota
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Burial record:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/59013134/jacob-myhre
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob and Mathea left Norway by sailing ship in 1867 on a journey that took 13 weeks. They then had a long trek by train and ox cart to reach Minnesota. They came to Fillmore County which was already settled by many of their count
2 CONC rymen. They settled on a farm near Lanesboro and resided there for 15 years, attempting to raise their family after years of grasshopper infestations that stripped the fields of harvest. Records find them in Pilot Mound Township n
2 CONC ot far from Lanesboro.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob was born March 1, 1842 on the farm Myre, Øvre (Upper), Fåvang, Ringebu, Norway. Mathea was born May 5, 1840, on Sør-Vekkom, Ringebu (South). They married April 1, 1867 in Ringebu and must have left for America shortly after 
2 CONC their marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT While in Fillmore County four of their children died. Mathea died August 18, 1876 from childbirth. The child died shortly after wards and they are buried at the Root Prairie Lutheran Church cemetery. It is said that they are burie
2 CONC d by the graves of the three other young children who had died in previous years. Three remaining children were daughter Janna who was a year old at the time and her twin brothers a few years older.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1880 census Jacob and the three surviving children appear in the census for Pilot Mound Township. Jacob is also listed as a member of the Root Prairie Lutheran Church. In 1882 Jacob decided to move West. According to family
2 CONC  stories, he felt that Fillmore County was getting crowded. If he planned to expand his head of cattle he needed more pasture and hay land. And there was the constant reminder of his wife and children.
2 CONT
2 CONT They traveled by covered wagon driven by oxen with a head of 20 Shorthorn cattle. The trip took twenty days. On the way they stopped for a drink of water at a log cabin on the Sheyenne River and the three children contracted dipht
2 CONC heria. The twin sons, Andor and Ole, died and were buried along the river. Jacob and his last remaining child Janna continued and found a place to build a dug out home where they lived for many years until Jacob could construct a 
2 CONC home. Janna married Mathias Rorvig also from Ringebu and they lived with Jacob on the farm that they eventually took over. It became known as the Rorvig Farm of Binford, North Dakota, and remained with the family for over 100 year
2 CONC s. Jacob died January 16, 1925 and is buried at Binford Cemetery, Griggs County, North Dakota. Janna, known as Jennie, died May 5, 1952 and is also buried there.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 13, 1982 family and friends celebrated the 100th Anniversary of this "Century Farm".
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Utvandringa til Amerika frå Ringebu by Einar Hordhaugen. (immigration list from Ringebu)
2 CONT
2 CONT 100 Year Anniversary announcement of Rorvig farm. Text by Lloyd George Melgard, grandson of Jacob Myhre's brother, Lars Myhre Melgard.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Janna, the Girl Without a Childhood," by Ruth Tweed. Printed by Hillsboro Banner for the Binford Centennial Celebration.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ringebu Historielag article and photos.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob was 1 year old when his father died from an accidental drowning in the nearby river.  He was 24 when he emigrated, the same year he got married.  On his death certificate is listed father Ole Myhre who would have been his mo
2 CONC ther's second husband and would have been the one who raised him as his father Hans died just one year after his death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Myhre and the Rorvig Farm
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans Myhre’s Uncle, Jacob Hanson Myhre, along with his wife Mathea emigrated in 1867, coming to Lanesboro, Minnesota, where they settled on a farm and for fifteen years he resided there attempting to raise his family despite year 
2 CONC after year of grasshopper infestations that stripped the fields of his harvest.  In 1876 his wife died in childbirth and she and the child were buried at Lanesboro by the graves of three other infants who had died in the previous 
2 CONC years. Jacob Hansen Myhre and Mathea Arnesdatter Berg
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob and Mathea left Norway by sailing ship in 1867 on a journey that took 13 weeks.  They then had a long trek by train and ox cart to reach Minnesota.  They settled on a farm near Lanesboro* and resided there for 15 years, atte
2 CONC mpting to raise their family after years of grasshopper infestations that stripped the fields of harvest.    
2 CONT
2 CONT While in Fillmore County four of their children died. Mathea died August 18, 1876 from childbirth.  The child died shortly after wards and they are buried at the Root Prairie Lutheran Church cemetery.  It is said that they are bur
2 CONC ied by the graves of the three other young children who had died in previous years.  Three remaining children were daughter Janna who was a year old at the time and her twin brothers a few years older.
2 CONT
2 CONT Six years later, in 1882, Jacob decided to try his luck in the Dakota Territory, so with his nine-year-old twin sons and his seven-year old daughter, he started off in a covered wagon with his household goods, twenty head of his S
2 CONC horthorn cattle, etc. on that long trip that took him twenty days.  On the way, they stopped for a drink of water at a log cabin on the Sheyenne River and there found that dreaded disease, diphtheria.  The twin sons died of it and
2 CONC  were buried along the river.  He continued on with his daughter Janna to an area north of what became Binford in what was then Dakota Territory.  He dug out the side of a hill a rough and primitive home for them and they lived in
2 CONC  it for many years until he could construct a home.  Janna married in 1896 to Mathias Rorvig and the farm became known as the Rorvig Farm.  On June 13, 1982 the Rorvig family celebrated the 100th Anniversary of the farm.* 
2 CONT
2 CONT Taken from Utvadringa til Amerika fra Ringebu, by Einar Hovdhaugen and the souvenir centennial program written by Lloyd George Melgard, grandson of Jacob Myhre’s brother, Lars Myhre. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This picture on the cover of the book by Hovdhaugen commemorates the emigration to America of so many Norwegians. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Story of Jacob and Janna Myhre by Ruth Tweed* for the Binford Centennial Celebration
2 CONT
2 CONT ”I wrote the following story after an interview with Clara Rorvig, Jacob Myhre’s oldest granddaughter.”  
2 CONT
2 CONT JANNA, THE GIRL WITHOUT A CHILDHOOD
2 CONT
2 CONT Outside the sod home on the hillside all things are still.  Not a sound or shadow gave life to the Dakota landscape.  The cattle were settles quietly and no glimmer of light showed from the dugout.  Inside the house an eight-year-
2 CONC old girl huddled under a quilt and prayed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her request was simple.  “Kjara Gud velsign og bever pappa og send ham heim snart.” (Dear God, bless and keep Papa and send him home soon.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Papa had left yesterday, driving the hay wagon behind the oxen.  The trip was long—fifty miles to Mayville, where he could take care of his business and bring back the meager supplies they needed.  Janna wished that she could go a
2 CONC long but papa would be away two or three days and someone must stay home to watch the cows and take care of the house.  She was a big girl and Papa knew that she could do it.  She must not let him know how frightened she was to be
2 CONC  left alone.
2 CONT
2 CONT If a stranger came by, which was unlikely, she would hide until he went away.  She often thought about Indians.  The Indians here seemed friendly and peaceful but Janna remembered stories about the Indian uprising in southern Minn
2 CONC esota.  That happened shortly before Papa and Mama came to Fillmore County but people still talked about it.  The children listened to the stories and repeated them to each other in hushed whispers.  Sometimes Janna’s brother woul
2 CONC d tease her.  They liked to do that-but of-if they were only with her now!  She wouldn’t mind the teasing one bit!
2 CONT
2 CONT Janna and her father lived in the dugout for almost a year.  It was such a long time.  She could hardly believe that she had lived in Minnesota with two brothers.  It didn’t seem fair that Mama had all the other children in Heaven
2 CONC  with her when Janna and Papa were alone but Papa said they mustn’t question God’s way.  She didn’t remember her mother or the sister and brothers they had left beside their mother in the cemetery near Lanesboro, Minnesota.
2 CONT
2 CONT Janna was the daughter of Jacob Myhre, an early pioneer in Griggs County, North Dakota.  He came to his homestead in a covered wagon, pulled by oxen, and driving a herd of twenty cattle.  The trip from Norway took thirteen weeks a
2 CONC nd then a long trek by train and ox cart to reach Minnesota.  In 1882 he decided to move West.  Fillmore County was getting crowded and at the time there was much talk and excitement about land available in Dakota Territory.  Ther
2 CONC e was a chance for a new life.  The entire trip took three weeks and four days.  
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the first settlers in Willow Township was Jacob Myhre.  He was born in Ringebu, Gudbrandsdalen, Norway, on March 7, 1843. Griggs County Historical Society
2 CONT
2 CONT He became a citizen of the United States October 29, 1894. John Oie and Knute Thompson were the witnesses. Jacob Myhre was a member of West Prairie Lutheran Church.  Janna was in the first class to be confirmed there, officiated b
2 CONC y Reverend Larson in 1889.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later he transferred to Trinity Lutheran at Binford of which he was a member until his death on January 13, 1925.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Binford Cemetery Binford Griggs County North Dakota, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Find A Grave Memorial# 590131
2 CONT
2 CONT Some say he emigrated in 1866 per passenger list, first to Quebec.
2 CONT
2 CONT ED 79 Willow, T.147-148-R.60-61 Townships, Griggs, North Dakota, United States 1900
