2 DATE 9 MAR 1991
2 ADDR
3 CITY Minneapolis
3 STAE MN
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/3475647/leif-j-melgaard
2 CONT
2 CONT Leif was honored with the tile "National Heritage Fellow" by the National Endowment for the Arts in 1985:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.arts.gov/honors/heritage/fellows/fellow.php?id=1985_06
2 CONT
2 CONT bio in National Heritage site Leif Melgaard was born January 15, 1899, in Sor-Fron, Gudbrandsdal, Norway, into a peasant family, and spent his childhood working on the homestead. With the encouragement of an uncle, a talented loca
2 CONC l wood carver, he began to carve as a child. Melgaard's father discouraged it as a waste of time. "He was a slave driver, my father was, so I didn't have much time for carving, only in the evenings," Melgaard said.
2 CONT
2 CONT At age 17, he enrolled at the Craft School of the Museum of Industrial Arts at Dakka, Norway. The six-week course was his only formal training. It included two weeks of cabinetmaking and two weeks of carving under the tutelage of 
2 CONC Lillevik, the master Gudbrandsdal carver of the time. Gudbrandsdal carving is a traditional Norwegian style that is elaborately improvised, imaginative, and technically very difficult. It centers on the acanthus leaf and vine moti
2 CONC f and includes grotesque animals and other figures.
2 CONT
2 CONT Melgaard came to the United States in 1920, assuming that he could continue his artistic training. But opportunities were limited. After several years in the farming region of western Minnesota, he settled in Minneapolis in 1926. 
2 CONC There he found employment as a carpenter and cabinetmaker at the Lake Street Sash and Door Company. He remained there until his retirement in 1964. He continued carving at home and on occasion at work, when commissions involving o
2 CONC rnamental carving were received. One of his best-known pieces done for the company is the altar at the Norwegian Lutheran Memorial Church. After his retirement Melgaard devoted all his time to carving.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also took great pride in his work outside the traditional style. One such piece was a relief panel carved from mahogany in the 1940s and featuring an Italianate rococo design. "I was working with some Swiss and Germans, you kno
2 CONC w, and they were always talking about how clumsy Scandinavian wood carving was," he said. "I just wanted to show them."
2 CONT
2 CONT Melgaard used a variety of materials, including birch, oak, mahogany, maple, apple, and buckthorn. He went to great lengths to find the right wood for particular objects. Several spoons and bowls, for instance, were made of wood h
2 CONC e cut from a tree where the stump merges with the trunk so that the pieces would feature the contrasting textures.
2 CONT
2 CONT On a visit to his homeland in 1980, Melgaard found a birch spoon he had begun carving before he emigrated. He took it back to Minneapolis and completed it. The spoon was later presented to King Olav V of Norway when he visited the
2 CONC  United States. Melgaard was also proud of a small oval box with a dragon on its lid that he began in Norway and completed in America.
2 CONT
2 CONT His carving has been displayed at Norwegian gatherings around the state, and several pieces were included in an exhibition at Vesterheim, the Norwegian American museum in Decorah, Iowa. Many students of the tradition say that Melg
2 CONC aard's work would be better known if he had been willing to sell and exhibit more, and they rate him equal or superior to the finest European carvers. Apparently it was difficult for him to overcome the widespread characterization
2 CONC  of "whittling" as an idle activity and to recognize his own work as an art form. He was also reluctant to teach. "I don't have any workshop to teach in," he said. "And besides, they're always trying to learn my secrets." The Minn
2 CONC esota Carver, Leif Melgaard
2 CONT
2 CONT Leif J. Melgaard and Sue’s grandmother, Eva Myhre Kuefler, were second cousins and visited each other often as they both lived in the Twin Cities area. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in 1899 in Gudbrandsdalen, Norway and died in Minneapolis in 1991.  He received his training in woodcarving in Norway and after emigrating from Sør-Fron in 1920 he eventually worked in the cabinet department of the Lak
2 CONC e Street Sash and Door Company in Minneapolis until his retirement. Since then he made and decorated furniture and smaller wood objects in the finest traditions of Norwegian Baroque and Italian Renaissance carving. . He is one of 
2 CONC two traditional woodcarvers who were key to the revival of the art of Norwegian woodcarving and, although he never taught, he became a model to younger carvers primarily with his classical acanthus carving. Gubrandsdalen, where Le
2 CONC if came from, is a section of Norway famous for its acanthus carving.  
2 CONT
2 CONT From an article published by the Vesterheim Museum for a special Norwegian Wood Day museum event that included Leif’s carvings and drawings: (1991):
2 CONT
2 CONT A “National Treasure” 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Minneapolis artist Leif Melgaard died in March at the age of 92, the world lost a dominant figure in woodcarving in the Norwegian tradition. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The ten carvings in the exhibition cover a broad range of styles, including traditional Norwegian acanthus, Italian Renaissance acanthus, Art Deco Modern from the 1930’s, and figure carving.   							
2 CONT
2 CONT There are a number of items on display or in storage at the Vesterheim Museum and a large Norwegian porridge container (grotamber) at the Minnesota History Museum, currently in storage.  Many of these items were donated by Leif hi
2 CONC mself, purchased from his sister Anne, or donated by the Melgaard estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT The carvings are supplemented with a selection of drawings presented to the museum by the Melgaard estate that reveal that Melgaard was a highly trained academic artist as well as a woodcarver.  
2 CONT
2 CONT While employed in Minneapolis with the Lake Street Sash and Door Company, he carved the altar for the Norwegian Memorial Lutheran Church in Minneapolis.  Through his long association with Vesterheim he received its Distinguished C
2 CONC arver Plaque in 1982.  In the early 1980s, the museum helped obtain a spoon carved by Melgaard for presentation to His Majesty Olav V, King of Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1985 Melgaard officially became a “National Treasure” when he was one of twelve U.S. artists to receive the National Heritage Award from the National Endowment for the Arts “ in recognition of extraordinary accomplishment and a
2 CONC chievement in a traditional arts field.” He was the first Minnesota to be so honored.
2 CONT
2 CONT As part of his will Leif donated funds to Vesterheim that were used to create a Woodcarvers Workshop which is named for him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From 2013 Minnesota State Fair Creative Activities: "Perry and Sheila McGowan, Forest Lake, Minn., will award $50 and a rosette in honor of Leif J. Melgaard for Exceptional Wood Carving , with special recognition for acanthus or o
2 CONC ther hand carving in the Scandinavian traditions." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Name:	Leif Melgaard Arrival Date:	5 May 1920 Birth Date:	abt 1899 Birth Location:	Nrw Birth Location Other:	søndre fron Age:	21 Gender:	Male Ethnicity/ Nationality:	Scandinavian Port of Departure:	Kristiania, Norway Port of Arriva
2 CONC l:	New York, New York Ship Name:	Stavangerfjord Destination..Warren MN Came to Warren MN with a friend of Andreas Melgard, Mathias Olsen Kvikstadhagen (also spelled Kvigstadhagen) Newspaper Warren Sheaf said it took them two weeks
2 CONC  minus 2 hrs. to travel there.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Vintage Wooden Keepsake Box Norse Bentwood Tine Nordic Style ...Leif Melgaard was a noted Norwegian-American woodcarver known for crafting traditional Norwegian bentwood boxes (often called tine or svepask). He famously created an
2 CONC  oval bentwood box featuring a dragon carving, started in Norway and completed in the U.S., with his work exhibited at Vesterheim, the National Norwegian-American Museum and Heritage
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.arts.gov/honors/heritage/leif-melgaard#:~:text=Melgaard%20was%20also%20proud%20of,American%20museum%20in%20Decorah%2C%20Iowa.
