1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Star-Mirror, Moscow, Idaho, December 14, 1911: "As the result of the unexpected death of Mrs. H. Melgard, this morning, citizens of Moscow, without exception are stricken with a feeling of deep gloom. Although 
2 CONC Mrs. Melgard had been ill for a short time the community failed to realize the seriousness of her trouble, and her death was altogether unlooked for.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mrs. Melgard, nee Winnie Alice Kennedy, was born October 1, 1885 at Garfield, Wn., the daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Kennedy. In that city she grew to womanhood and was beloved by those who knew her.
2 CONT
2 CONT "On July 15, 1906, she was married to Hawkin Melgard, at Kennewick, Wn., and after a brief honeymoon, came to Moscow, a beautiful and charming bride, and from that time has made her home in this city.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The mother of three small children, Mrs. Melgard, was most devoted to her home. Although a member of the Order of the Eastern Star, her home duties prevented her taking an active part in the work of that order, as they prevented 
2 CONC other social activity, although Mrs. Melgard was a charming hostess, and was ever a welcome guest in the social affairs she was able to attend. She was a devout member of the Christian church.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The deceased leaves a husband, three small daughters, Alice Gladys, aged 4; Helen Winfred, aged 3, and Thelma Solverg, aged 16 months, together with her sorrowing father, Thomas Kennedy, of Canby, Ore.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her family can be traced back through Bacon name who were emigrants from England in the mid 1600's.  They also emigrated to Canada. 
