1 NAME Iver Eriksen /Romsås/
2 GIVN Iver Eriksen
2 _MARNM Romsås
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 22 OCT 1823
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 DEC 1863
2 PLAC Union General Hospital
2 ADDR
3 CITY Chattanooga
3 STAE Tennessee
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Birth:	Oct 22 1823, Ringebu, Oppland, Norway 
2 CONT Christening:	Oct 26 1823, Ringebu, Oppland, Norway 
2 CONT Marriage:	June 1 1852, Ringebu, Oppland, Norway 
2 CONT Emigration:	from Norway 1853
2 CONT Marriage:	Oct 23 1857, Viroqua, Vernon, Wisconsin, United States
2 CONT Death:	Dec 1 1863, Chattanooga, Hamilton, Tennessee, United States
2 CONT Burial:	Dec 1863, Chattanooga, Hamilton, Tennessee, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Died of dysentery, a common ailment of soldiers in the Civil War.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Marit Gulbrandsdatter Romsaas (born Strande), first wife, emigrated with him and died shortly after their arrival in Wisconsin.  Karen Ericksdatter Romsaas (born Thorsgaard), second wife, from Fåvang, married in Wisconsin.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Iver_Romsaas 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1853 Iver Erikson Sygard Romsaas, his wife Marit Gudbrandsdatter Strand and daughter Ane Iversdatter Romsaas immigrated to America. As they came to Wisconsin, Iver’s wife Marit died, leaving Iver and 2-year-old daughter Ane. Re
2 CONC cords show that Peter P. Hektoen became Ane's guardian.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 June 1860, the 1860 Census of the United States shows Iver Erickson living in Bad Axe (now Vernon) County, Wisconsin. His second wife, immigrant Karen Eriksdatter Teige (nee Thorsgaard) and Karen’s children are listed living
2 CONC  together with Iver Erikson.  Private Iver Ericksen Romsaas, Union Army
2 CONT
2 CONT In Bad Axe, Wisconsin on 16 December 1861 immigrant Iver Ericksen Sygard Romsaas enlisted in Company E of the 15th Regiment of the Wisconsin Volunteers. This famous regiment of the Union Army was called the “Scandinavian Regiment”
2 CONC .  Although many Scandinavians served on both the Union and Confederate sides of the war, the 15th was the only all-scandinavian regiment on either side.  Private Romsaas was mustered into service in Madison, Wisconsin on 6 Januar
2 CONC y 1862.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is the history of the 15th Regiment while Iver served in it:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Snowy Departure'''. On March 1st, 1862, the 15th left Wisconsin in a rain storm that turned to snow. Shortly thereafter their railroad train got stuck in the snow and the 15th had to dig it out.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Earthquake'''. On June 6, 1862, the 15th's camp at Island No. 10, Tennessee, was struck by an earthquake which lasted some 3 minutes.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Grueling March'''.  Starting from Iuka, Mississippi on August 21, 1862, the 15th marched some 400 miles to Louisville, Kentucky in 37 days, the last 2 weeks on half rations with little water.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Dusty Trail'''. Sergeant Rollin Olson described the dust stirred up by the Army's September 1862 passage through drought-stricken Kentucky as being "as thick as a thick fog."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Perryville/Chaplin Hills'''. This bloody October 8, 1862, Union victory in Kentucky was the 15th's first big battle. Despite forcing the enemy back more than a mile, none of the regiment's soldiers were killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Cannon Captured'''. The 15th charged a Rebel artillery battery at Knob Gap, near Nashville, Tennessee, on December 26, 1862, capturing a brass cannon and 3 prisoners.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Stone River/Murfreesboro'''. The 15th suffered 36% casualties at this frosty December 30-31, 1862, Union victory in Tennessee. They suffered severely from the cold and a lack of rations during and after the battle. Colonel Heg 
2 CONC and the 15th were both cited for their bravery.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''First Across'''. In the Chickamauga Campaign the 15th became the first Union regiment to advance south over the Tennessee River, crossing in pontoon boats during a daring early morning assault on August 28, 1863.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chickamauga'''. The 15th suffered 63% casualties at this September 19-20, 1863, Union defeat in Georgia -- the war's second bloodiest battle. Colonel Heg was mortally wounded and all of the 15th's field officers were killed, wo
2 CONC unded, or captured. The next morning just 75 men answered role call.
2 CONT
2 CONT Company E, 15th Regiment muster rolls state that Private Romsaas was detached in the Brigade Provost Guard on 19 June 1863 by order of Colonel Heg.  On 24 October Private Romsaas was sent to the General Field Hospital in Chattanoo
2 CONC ga, Tennessee. On 1 December 1863 Private Iver Ericksen Sygard Romsaas died in the Chattanooga General Field Hospital of chronic diarrhea. He was survived by his second wife, the widow Karen Eriksdatter Theige (nee Thorsgard) and 
2 CONC daughter Ane Iversdatter Sygard Romsaas from his first marriage.
