1 NOTE {geni:about_me}   Hans Erikson Myhre
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Tromsnes, Fåvang Parish, Ringebu
2 CONT
2 CONT Romsås, Myhre, and Segelstad Family Names
2 CONT
2 CONT (Myhre also spelled Myre and pronounced like mirror but with an a at the end)
2 CONT
2 CONT The name is derived from myr (peat swamp) and indicates moist ground.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans Eriksen Myhre emigrated to the USA at the age of 19 leaving behind his ancestral home and family. His family can be traced as far back as the 1300's, living on various farms in the Fåvang/Ringebu area and neighboring communit
2 CONC ies throughout the Gudbrandsdalen Valley.  One of his ancestors on his mother's side was Kristen Listad, the famous woodcarver from Sør-Fron. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans took advantage of the education he received both in Norway and the US, married, raised a family and became a prosperous businessman and banker during a time when the upper Midwest was rapidly growing. Then hard times fell on 
2 CONC the bank during the early years of the Depression and Hans had to start over, spending his final years operating a gas station in St. Paul, Minnesota.   
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born July 20, 1869 in Tromsnes, Fåvang Parish, the oldest of five siblings.  Tromsnes is a very small town in Fåvang Parish. His father was born Erik Hansen Romsås (from framigard Romsås) but after Erik's father bought Øvre
2 CONC  Myhre their surname became Myhre.  His mother was Eli Guldbransdatter Segelstad.  In 1869 Erik built a general store at Tromsnes where Hans was born, and since this was not a farm, they kept the Myhre name.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Hans emigrated to the US he used the family name Myhre.  He left May 18, 1888 on the S/S Angelo from Christiania to England and then to his final destination, Black River Falls, Wisconsin.*  He had received an education at th
2 CONC e County or District School as a young man and must have seen an opportunity to use this education in the US. There was also an opportunity to join a number of cousins and uncles from the Melgard family who had emigrated earlier. 
2 CONC  His uncle Lars, who was Erik's twin brother, married Ane Melgard, purchased Sygard Melgard, and took the Melgard name. All of his sons emigrated to the US. This would be the same family that Leif Melgard is connected to.  Another
2 CONC  uncle, Jacob Myhre, settled on the Rørivg farm in Binford, ND. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the Melgard cousins and an uncle had come to Black River Falls, Wisconsin in 1876 and this is where Hans first lived.  He entered high school to learn English and took a business course. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He then went to Madison, Minnesota where he was employed in the local lumberyard and met Emma Brecto who was visiting her sister.  They married February 15, 1894 in her hometown at Spring Prairie Church, Columbia County, Wisconsin
2 CONC .  Her parents were among the early emigrants in that part of Wisconsin, having come from Telemark in the late 1840's. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After their marriage they moved to Badger, Iowa, where Hans became manager of the J. H. Quell lumber company.  All four of their children, Eva, Hazel, Ernest, and Erick were born there.  After ten years in Badger they moved to New
2 CONC folden, in northwestern Minnesota, and met up with several of the Melgard cousins who by this time had a chain of banks in the area.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1904 Hans became the cashier of the newly chartered Farmers State Bank. At the time Newfolden was pioneer territory and Hans was among the early settlers and a community leader.  He bought a track of land which became the town 
2 CONC site and had cattle shipped in.  He had to have the cattle hauled by oxen to Newfolden from Thief River Falls, the nearest railroad town at the time.  Once the railroad came through the town began to grow and Hans was among a grou
2 CONC p of individuals who founded the first Norwegian Lutheran Church, was a Village Trustee of the newly incorporated Newfolden Village, and Chairman of the School Board.  When he and Emma built their home on the riverbank it was said
2 CONC  that people came from miles around to see it. All the farmers lived in log cabins and many had never seen hardwood floors.  It was a beautiful home on the riverbank. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From an article in the Warren Sheaf newspaper Sept. 1915, regarding Newfolden the townsite was platted by the Soo road land department and was afterwards purchased by Andrew Korstad and H. E. Myhre.  There are 75 lots in the busin
2 CONC ess and residence zone still unsold
2 CONT
2 CONT During this time the town and the banking business were growing and the Farmers State Bank merged with the People's State Bank to become the Farmers and Merchants Bank. The bank was robbed by seven men who used nitroglycerin to br
2 CONC eak open the bank and escaped by handcar on the rails. The bank was hit by hard times when farmers could not pay their  debts and with the panic during the early days of the Depression the bank was forced to close.  Hans lost ever
2 CONC ything he had and sold his home and holdings in an attempt to satisfy the creditors.  The bank never reopened.  Left Newfolden in 1928. 1930 census both Hans and Emma living with Erick and wife Charlotte..Erick is a milkman and Ch
2 CONC arlotte a bookkeeper at same business.  Living in Minneapolis  2820 E Minnehaha Pkwy
2 CONT
2 CONT He was now nearly 60 years old and thankfully his family was all grown.  It was hardly a time to start over but he and Emma, after living for a short time with their daughter Eva and her family,  moved to St. Paul, Minnesota where
2 CONC  Hans operated a gas station in downtown St. Paul with the help of his son Erick. (see addresses and name of gas station later in article) Eventually daughter Eva and her family moved to nearby Forest Lake, Minnesota so that she c
2 CONC ould also be nearby. Both their home and the gas station were near the St. Paul Cathedral and Selby Street car Tunnel.  Their son Ernest had passed away in 1932 at the age of 30 and daughter Hazel married and was living in Chicago
2 CONC . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandchildren remember visiting Hans and Emma along with their mother Eva in St. Paul and could never understand what was being said as they spoke Norwegian.  Hans loved to smoke his pipe and did not have much to say in these conv
2 CONC ersations except Ja, Ja! He was remembered as a very nice and quiet gentleman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans died in 1952 and Emma in 1951.  They are buried along with their son Ernest at the Oakwood Cemetery just north of downtown St. Paul. https://oaklandcemeterymn.com/map/Block_102S.asp Their daughter  Hazel died in 1951. Block: 
2 CONC 102 Lot: 474
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Back in Fåvang, Norway, shortly after Hans left for the US his father bought his wife's family farm, Nedre Segelstad and took the name Segelstad.  Hans had four siblings, Emilie, Marit, Marie, and Gudbrand who all took the Segelst
2 CONC ad name. Gudbrand took over the farm in 1926 and then after his death Marie became owner. But she took over the farm in 1942 only on the condition that if Hans wanted to return and settle on the farm she had to sell it to him as h
2 CONC e was the oldest and rightful person to inherit the farm.  But he never returned to his homeland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One sibling, Emilie,  became a teacher and was the main link in Norway for Hans and his family  through letters and she sent some beautiful handwork to their daughter Eva.   Sadly she died in the flu epidemic of 1918 in Narvik. No
2 CONC rway.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1951 Marie sold the Nedre Segelstad farm to one of Eva and Erick's second cousins living in Norway. Gudbrand and Marie left no heirs and after their death Eva and Erick received a small inheritance from the sale of the  farm. F
2 CONC rom the inheritance they donated a number of items that came from the farm to the Maighaugen Museum in Lillehammer.  There were also a number of items that had previously been donated from the Segelstad farm to the museum in 1923 
2 CONC including many items of the previously mentioned woodcarver Kristen Listad.  The Segelstad farm was said to be the oldest home in Gudbrandsdalen.  One item from the collection, a birch tankard, was then given in 1927 to the Vester
2 CONC heim Museum in Decorah, Iowa and is part of the Luther College collection. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The general store built by Han's father, Erik, in Tromsnes remains and has gone through many remodels.    
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately neither Hans nor any of his children visited his homeland.  International travel was  a luxury at that time that many could not afford.  Daughter Eva spoke Norwegian and carried on many of the Norwegian traditions ma
2 CONC inly through her cooking and the Christmas holiday.  
2 CONT
2 CONT It was not until 1985 that any of Han's direct ancestors traveled back to his homeland.  Granddaughter Aileen (Eva's daughter) Melbostad and husband Merton visited Norway and spent a couple of days in the Ringebu area.  Jon Ødegår
2 CONC d, Lief Melgard's nephew, was a gracious host, showed them the farms associated with Han's family and introduced them to family members. Aileen remembers being served a meal that reminded her of the food we have for our Christmas 
2 CONC Eve meal.  They also toured the Maighaugen Museum where the staff kept pointing to items, telling them that these are from her  family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Han's family has been linked by others in Norway who have contributed to this site with many ancestors going back to Viking times.  
2 CONT
2 CONT *from Norway Genealogy facebook.."Hans E. Myhre arrived in Boston aboard the "S. S. Catalonia" on 5 Jun 1888. The Catalonia had sailed from Liverpool. His name is not completely readable on the passenger list, that is why you will
2 CONC  not find it in any index. The only way to find him is to look for people who left Norway on the same day with the same ship and line. I found enough people on the list to confirm that this is indeed your Hans E. Myhre. I will pos
2 CONC t the record on this page." #436 is Hans E. Myhre, 19 years old. He arrived in Boston on 5 Jun 1888 aboard the "S.S. Catalonia". Letter from Hans back to his family confirms the Catalonia from Liverpool to Boston.  He was supposed
2 CONC  to be on a ship to New York but the agent said no, you are on this one.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Warren sheaf., March 17, 1910 For Sale 100 cedar telephone poles, 20 ft. and longer, for sale at 50 cents each. H. E. Myhre, Newfolden, Minn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Warren sheaf., April 29, 1909 H. L. Wood and L. M. Olson, of Warren J. C. Figenskau, of Oslo and E. Myhre, of Newfolden, are the Marshall County bankers, who at tended the bankers' convention of the 9th district held at Crookston 
2 CONC yester dav.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1931 residence 314 W. 4th. Street St. Paul along with Emma and son Erick M.  Both are station operators with City Service Oil. See photo of Hans sitting on front steps. 1944 at 203 5th Ave. Apt. 2 with Emma.  Son Erick and Victori
2 CONC a at 944 E. Orange Ave.  Hans E. and Sons listed gas station at 255 W. 5th. Believe this to be near the corner of Smith Ave. and W. Fifth Street. Not far from where Kellogg and Smith intersect.   Ramsey County Plate 004 Atlas.   R
