1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From an obituary in "Rockbridge Weekly & Alleghany Journal", http://www.rockbridgeweekly.com/obits/obit.php?action=3&id=741
2 CONT
2 CONT "Virginia Tucker Melgaard
2 CONT
2 CONT Worked To Improved Lives Of Relocated American Indians
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia Tucker Melgaard, 87, died on October 29, 2004 at the Mayflower in Lexington. Her parents were the late Col. Albert S. J. Tucker and Mary Sames Tucker. She was the sister of Col. Albert S. J. Tucker, Jr. of Lexington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia was born August 8, 1917 in Laredo, Texas. Her father’s Army career took the family to many stations around the country, but the Tucker children spent summers and holidays with their extended family at Col Alto in Lexingto
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1936 Col. Tucker took charge of the ROTC program at the University of California, Berkeley. It was there, while attending the University, that Virginia met Thomas L. Melgaard, whom she married in 1937. Her parents retired in 19
2 CONC 39 to Lexington. The Melgaards remained in California where they brought up their family in Berkeley and in Southern California.
2 CONT
2 CONT Throughout her life, Virginia was active in parishes of the Episcopal Church. She was particularly concerned about improving the lives of relocated American Indians and volunteered many hours of service to that community. After mo
2 CONC ving to Southern Californa, she took medical training and served as a nurse in several hospitals.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1999, four years after her husband passed away, Virginia returned to her roots, in Virginia. She lived for a while at Leisure World, Lansdowne and then at the Mayflower in Lexington, where she continued to shine her special lig
2 CONC ht. She will be missed by her friends and family. Survivors, in addition to her brother and family, include a son, Tom Melgaard and his wife, Janet of Bluemont, Va; a son, John Melgaard and his wife, Cindy, of Coeur d’Alene, ID; a
2 CONC  daughter, Karen Melgaard Millar, of Costa Mesa, CA; six grandchildren and seven great grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Services are scheduled for 11 a.m. on Saturday, November 13, 2004 at the R. E. Lee Memorial Episcopal Church, 123 W. Washington St., Lexington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrangements are being handled by the Harrison Funeral Home & Crematory, Lexington."
