1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Mankell Family History
2 CONT
2 CONT Return to Home Page
2 CONT
2 CONT "A Pioneer Woman Found Her Place in the New World"
2 CONT The story of Anne Christopherson Olson Holter Written by Orlynn Mankell
2 CONT
2 CONT Storyteller and historian, Orlynn Mankell From the Territorial Journal, January 18, 1985
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 1, 1862, the America-bound sailing vessel, the Nordlyset, lay along the pier in Christiania (later Oslo), Norway. Throughout the day she had been taking on passengers, all of whom could now be referred to as "immigrants," t
2 CONC hat well known expression that would become famous in accounts of many years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among those boarding the Nordlyset on this important, much-prepared-for day was immigrant Kristoffer Olson, along with his wife, Anne, and their seven children. Taken on board were several chests and trunks, all plainly marked "Kr
2 CONC istoffer Olson." Like the others, Kristoffer was in the process of cutting off all ties behind him and would now find a new beginning for himself and his family in the New World. The record would state that a total of 160 passenge
2 CONC rs would board the ship on this day.
2 CONT
2 CONT A fairly large crowd had gathered to see the Nordlyset off, and now the last agonizing dockside farewells were geing completed. Lines to the pier were untied and the ship was pulled out into the harbor. Sails were hoisted. Soon th
2 CONC e ship was proceeding slowly down Oslo-fjord with her passengers now standing on deck looking their last on their homeland. Within a day or so the Nordlyset was rounding the southern end of Norway and then, with wind filling her s
2 CONC ails, she headed out into the perils of the North Atlantic.
2 CONT
2 CONT Norway gone Such was the departure of Anne and Kristoffer and family for the New World. Norway's rugged coastline was visible behind them for some time but it eventually disappeared. Norway, land of their birth, was gone forever. 
2 CONC On board the passengers settled down for a long voyage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vessels such as the Nordlyset were merchant ships and were not designed for passengers. All accommodations were makeshift and were in the cargo area below deck -- and where ventilation was poor. It was not quite the vacation cruis
2 CONC e as seen from today. Food would spoil. Water would become scarce. There were days of storm and others of intense fog and with no wind, there was hardly any progress for the day.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Norwegian newspaper in Madison, Wisc. carried the following information. Dateline: Quebec, Canada, June 15, 1862 -- "The Nordlyset has arrived today with 160 passengers from Norway. The first leg of this Norway-to-Minnesota jour
2 CONC ney was been completed. Then it was overland by rail and riverboat to Winona, Minn., and from there to the large Norwegian settlement in Fillmore County in southestern Minnesota.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Homestead Act was now in effect, but Kristoffer quickly discovered that he nevertheless had come at a very unfavorable time. The country was caught up in the American Civil War. Then, in August 1862, came the sudden and bloody
2 CONC  Dacohtah War, leaving western Minnesota closed to virtually all settlement -- where Kristoffer had intended to settle. And all land in and around Fillmore County had been taken. Kristoffer would have to wait. To make ends meet he
2 CONC  found a job.
2 CONT
2 CONT Time goes on The weeks lengthened into months, and then the months into years. Anne and Kristoffer were now the parents of 10 children. To help feed his large family, he fished in a stream that ran close to his house. But still th
2 CONC is continuing vision -- someday soon, a place of his own.
2 CONT
2 CONT The long Civil War was over. Then was to begin that famous westward migration in Minnesota. Kristoffer and his family certainly were to be part of it, and plans were being made. But it was now that there would be a drastic turn of
2 CONC  events. Kristoffer had not been feeling well for a while, and a few days later the family was thrown into a sudden, and totally unexpected, crisis. Kristoffer Olson died.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was a somber and startling development, but to die young was a part of the times. It would now all depend upon Anne, and one thing she was quick to make clear -- and 50 years later people still would remember what she declared 
2 CONC -- the trip was still on! And preparations continued.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Christopherson Olson (later Holter)
2 CONT
2 CONT Westward ho On a day in the summer of 1867, two ox-drawn covered wagons left the community of Arendal in northeastern Fillmore County and headed north into the wilderness. Destination: Kandiyohi County. Anne and her 10 children ha
2 CONC d said good-bye to their friends behind them as this long-awaited adventure was at last underway.
2 CONT
2 CONT It would prove to be one of the most remarkable covered wagon expeditions ever to occur in Minnesota. They camped at night along the trail, wherever night found them.
2 CONT
2 CONT They crossed areas blackened by fire. There were days of rain and mud. In one instance, one of the sons walked three or four miles in search of help in pulling the wagons out of deep mud -- but help he did not find. Not many miles
2 CONC  were covered that day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their record shows they camped and rested for two days on what is now the Minnesota State Fairgrounds, and four large, hard-working oxen probably also enjoyed the two-day break.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then it was on to St. Cloud, a visit to the land office there. And from there Anne set course to the southwest. This month-long covered wagon odyssey came to its conclusion when the family arrived in Arctander Township, Kandiyohi 
2 CONC County. It was now five years since the family had sailed down Oslo-fjord on the Nordlyset. Their own part of the New World was found at last -- a part that Kristoffer Olson was not permitted to see.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the State Fairgrounds today -- and where Anne made camp 117 years ago -- stands a metal figure depicting the Pioneer Woman. It could be Anne.
2 CONT
2 CONT A note from Carolyn Sowinski: There are various spellings for this pioneer: Anna/Anne; Christopherson/Christofferson; Olson and later Holter. Anne and Kristoffer's children were Olas Olson, Hans Holter, Rev. Karl (KC) Holter, Rev.
2 CONC  Christian Holter, Martin Holter, Otto Holter, Anna Holter, Julia Holter, Thea Holter, Karine Holter. Their last name was Olson and later Holter, which reflected the area where they lived in Norway. Olas is the only son who did no
2 CONC t change his name to Holter. Karl and Christian were Lutheran pastors; Anna married Rev. Kringle; Julia married Rev. Njust; Thea married J.A. Johnson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne, and sons Olas, Martin, Otto, Karl and Hans each had farms in the same area of Arctander Township, near the Lars Christopherson homestead. By the 1880s all the Holters, except Olas, had left the area for Minneapolis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Widow Anne Christofferson received her U.S. citizenship in 1874. Usually a woman derived citizenship through her husband, but because she was a widow, Anne applied directly with the District Court in Minnesota.
2 CONT
2 CONT Citizenship of Anne Christofferson, October 26, 1874. Witnesses included son Hans Olson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigration Stories of Lars and sister Anna Hjelle and Christopherson page Orlynn and Vivian Mankell page Webmaster: Carolyn Mankell Sowinski Last updated: February 8, 2013
